\ Benchmark: interned single-character strings.
\
\ `>char` and the single-codepoint string accessors return a shared one-byte
\ slice for ASCII codepoints (0..127) instead of allocating a fresh string per
\ call; non-ASCII codepoints still allocate a fresh UTF-8 string. This isolates
\ that effect: the same iteration count over an ASCII codepoint (interned) and a
\ non-ASCII codepoint (duped). The signal is total_bytes -- the ASCII loop pulls
\ far fewer bytes from the arena than the non-ASCII loop.
\
\ Run under `--benchmark` so the `benchmark` hash carries total_allocations /
\ total_bytes; the rows read "n/a" otherwise. Note total_allocations counts
\ backing-allocator calls and is insensitive here (the arena batches its
\ sub-allocations and refcounted churn dominates), which is exactly why the
\ tokenizer-workload benchmark cannot see this lever; total_bytes is the column
\ that moves.

iters: ( -- fixnum ) const [ 500000 ] ;

\ Benchmark `quot` and read one stat key; f when the key is absent (i.e. when
\ not run under --benchmark). The loop body is deterministic, so re-running
\ benchmark per key yields identical figures.
bench-key: ( quot key -- value ) [
  swap benchmark  \ key hash
  swap 2dup @has? [ @get ] [ 2drop f ] if
] ;

>na: ( x -- str ) [ dup f = [ drop "n/a" ] [ >string ] if ] ;

report: ( label quot -- ) [
  swap print-line  \ quot
  "  total_allocations=" print
  dup "total_allocations" bench-key >na print-line  \ quot
  "  total_bytes=" print
  "total_bytes" bench-key >na print-line
] ;

"=== >char single-character interning: ASCII (interned) vs non-ASCII (duped) ===" print-line
"iters: " print iters >string print-line
"" print-line

"ascii (codepoint 65, interned)" [ iters [ 65 >char drop ] times ] report
"" print-line
"wide  (codepoint 256, duped)" [ iters [ 256 >char drop ] times ] report
